          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does the thyroid do, and what goes wrong in hypothyroidism?",
          "answer": "The thyroid is a small gland in the neck. It makes mainly thyroxine, which the body converts into a more active hormone, triiodothyronine. Together these hormones set the speed at which most tissues in the body work, often called the metabolic rate. (Source: *Hypothyroidism (underactive thyroid)*, general clinical reference.)\n\nHypothyroidism means the gland does not make enough thyroid hormone. The pituitary gland, in the brain, monitors hormone levels and releases thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) to push the thyroid to work harder. When the thyroid is underperforming, TSH rises, often before the thyroid hormone level itself drops out of the normal range. In most cases the fault is in the thyroid itself (primary hypothyroidism). Less often, the pituitary or hypothalamus is the problem (central hypothyroidism). (Source: *Hypothyroidism (underactive thyroid)*.)\n\nDoctors usually start with a TSH blood test. If TSH is high, they measure free thyroxine to tell overt hypothyroidism (low free thyroxine) from subclinical hypothyroidism (free thyroxine still in the normal range). Thyroid peroxidase antibodies can show whether an autoimmune thyroiditis is the cause. (Source: *Hypothyroidism: which test comes first, and the monitoring interval after a change*, endocrinology guidance.)\n\nThe material I searched does not describe the symptoms of hypothyroidism or its treatment. If you have questions about how you feel or about a test result, please take them to a clinician who knows your history.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
